    "primary_subject_agency_id": "978d5b81-63f1-5f5d-bf83-4f108c15abcb",
    "summary": "EFF's year-in-review summarizes its 2025 investigations into Flock Safety's nationwide ALPR network, documenting agency searches tied to protests, racially biased searches targeting Romani people, and Texas deputies querying Flock data in an abortion-related investigation. The piece highlights resulting congressional and state investigations, an Illinois audit, an ACLU/EFF lawsuit against San Jose PD, municipal cancellations of Flock contracts, and concerns about Flock's expansion into audio 'distress' detection.",
    "key_quotes": [
      {
        "quote": "more than 80 law enforcement agencies used language perpetuating harmful stereotypes against Romani people when searching the nationwide Flock Safety ALPR network",
        "paragraph_idx": 6
      },
      {
        "quote": "A Johnson County official ran two searches with the note \"had an abortion, search for female.\" The second search probed 6,809 networks, accessing 83,345 cameras across nearly the entire country.",
        "paragraph_idx": 8
      },
      {
        "quote": "SJPD and other California law enforcement agencies searched San Jose's database 3,965,519 times",
        "paragraph_idx": 12
      },
      {
        "quote": "Illinois Secretary of State Alexi Giannoulias launched an audit after EFF research showed Flock allowed U.S. Customs and Border Protection to access Illinois data in violation of state privacy laws.",
        "paragraph_idx": 11
      }
    ],
